			"13": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "PHASE OFFSET & RESET\n\nPhase offset determines where in the phase the oscillator starts.\nReset Phase resets the oscillator to its starting point.\n\nAn oscillator with an amplitude of 1 will oscillate between -1 and 1.\nAt a phase offset of 0 its starting point would be 0.\nAt a phase offset of 0.25 its starting point would be 1.\nAt a phase offset of 0.75 its starting point would be -1.\n\nPhase offset is commonly used when there are multiple instances of a oscillator\nIn the example below we are sending a collection of float values between 0 and 1 to the phase offset. This instantiates the oscillator which controls the Y-position of the dots.\nBy doing this we make sure that every oscillator in the collection is \"out of sync\" with the others.\n\nAssignment: play around with the max value of the Linear node, try lowering and increasing the value.\n\nAssignment: Select the Linear and Sine node, hold the ALT key and drag out a copy. Now connect those nodes to the X input of the Float 2 node. Repeat the previous example and make something pretty.\n"
					}
				},
